// src/volume_device_slot.pdx — mount.pdxfs.PFS-11 (paideia-os#2578):
// real VOL_OP_QUERY_DEVICE_SLOT wire-through for both the mount-op
// dev_path construction AND the elevate-mint dev_cap argument.
//
// ======================================================================
// Context
// ======================================================================
//
// PFS-10 (mount.pdxfs v1.1.5) left TWO documented stop-gaps that this
// file closes:
//
//   STOP-GAP A (dev_path digit, src/mount_op.pdx):
//     `narrowed_volume_slot & 0xF` as the hex nibble spliced into
//     `/dev/pdxvol<H>`. A naive modulo-16 on a USER-SPACE cap-table
//     SLOT number -- not the backing device slot that the frozen ABI's
//     dev-path shim semantically encodes
//     (design/pdxfs/sys-mount-abi.md §2.8 is explicit: "PFS-11 lands
//     the real `VOL_OP_QUERY_DEVICE_SLOT` wire-through that builds
//     this string from the volume cap handle"). Two cap slots whose
//     low nibbles collide would both end up at the same dev_path even
//     if they back different kernel-side volume rows -- the current
//     SYS_MOUNT_ENODEV refuse covers the mis-mount case but any
//     adjacent operator diagnostics (`stat /dev/pdxvol4` audit-log
//     entries, etc.) would mis-attribute.
//
//   STOP-GAP B (dev_cap=0 fed to `MintWire::mint_wire_invoke`,
//               src/main.pdx line 340 and src/mint_wire.pdx header):
//     The /system-tier elevate-mint arm passes `xor rdi, rdi`
//     unconditionally for the KIND_BLKDEV `dev_cap` arg because the
//     M6-002 landing had no way to resolve a user-space KIND_VOLUME
//     cap-slot to its backing block-device cap. v1.1.1 flagged this
//     as "safe today because mint_wire_invoke's refuse-gate fires on
//     every reachable input before dev_cap is dereferenced", but the
//     day `mount_mw_elevate_slot` gets a real seed and the gate stops
//     firing, every /system mount would hit a null `dev_cap` inside
//     `vol_kind_mint_elevate` and fail opaquely.
//
// Both stop-gaps have the SAME root cause: no user-space resolver from
// a KIND_VOLUME cap-slot to the volume's backing device slot
// (= KIND_BLKDEV cap-slot per kernel kind_blkdev.pdx's unified family
// dispatch, where the kernel-side `volume_row_device_slot` returns
// header bits [39:24] = bdev_cap).
//
// Both stop-gaps have the SAME fix: `VOL_OP_QUERY_DEVICE_SLOT` (op
// ordinal 0), already landed kernel-side in `kind_volume.pdx`
// (R52.M5-001) as the first entry of the `cap_handler_volume`
// op-dispatcher's `vol_hn_device_slot` arm. The op routes through
// `volume_row_device_slot`, requires `R_VOL_INVOKE` (0x008), and
// returns the live-row device_slot in rax (or `VOL_DECODE_BAD` /
// `INVOKE_DENIED` / `INVOKE_UNSUPPORTED` on refusal).
//
// ======================================================================
// Why this is Path B (extend the KIND_VOLUME op surface) and not
// Path A (KIND_BLOCK_DEVICE `BD_OP_QUERY_SLOT`)
// ======================================================================
//
// PFS-11's scope allowed EITHER a `KIND_BLOCK_DEVICE` resolver on the
// backing device cap OR a `VOL_OP_QUERY_DEVICE_SLOT` op on the volume
// cap. A grep (2026-10-11) of the paideia-os kernel finds BOTH kinds
// landed: `KIND_BLKDEV` (0x42) with its unified family dispatcher in
// `cap_handler_blkdev.pdx` + `BDEV_OP_QUERY_FAMILY` /
// `BDEV_OP_QUERY_GEOM` / etc., AND `KIND_VOLUME` (0x1A0) with
// `VOL_OP_QUERY_DEVICE_SLOT` already defined. The direction of the
// resolution we need -- "I hold a volume cap; what's its backing block
// device?" -- is NATURALLY expressed on the volume cap: the volume
// knows its device_slot, the device does not know every volume that
// backs onto it. Attempting Path A would need the user to ALREADY
// hold a KIND_BLKDEV cap in the first place (and then query which
// volume uses it, inversely -- a one-to-many lookup kernel-side).
// Path B's semantics are the forward direction and already landed.
//
// This file therefore wires Path B. No kernel-side extension is
// needed; `VOL_OP_QUERY_DEVICE_SLOT` is already op 0 in
// `kind_volume.pdx`'s `cap_handler_volume` dispatcher.
//
// ======================================================================
// Return taxonomy
// ======================================================================
//
// `volume_device_slot_query` surfaces TWO classes of refusal distinct
// from a successful device_slot read:
//
//   VDS_OK (= device_slot in [0..65536)) -- any u64 whose high 48 bits
//     are zero. The real-world dense range today is [0..16) per
//     `_volume_table`'s 16-row cap (`kind_volume.pdx` line 220); nothing
//     in this file enforces a tighter bound because `kind_volume.pdx`
//     itself does not -- the kernel's `volume_row_device_slot` extracts
//     bits [39:24] (= 16-bit field) via `mov r10, 0xFFFF; and rax, r10`.
//     This file returns the kernel's answer verbatim.
//
//   VDS_ERR_DECODE   (= 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF) -- kernel returned
//     `VOL_DECODE_BAD` (volume row not live, bad target_ptr).
//
//   VDS_ERR_INVOKE   (= 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFD) -- kernel returned
//     `INVOKE_DENIED` (R_VOL_INVOKE not held). Numerically the same
//     constant `invoke.pdx` exports; recomputed here as a self-
//     documenting literal rather than a dependency.
//
//   VDS_ERR_UNSUP    (= 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFC) -- kernel returned
//     `INVOKE_UNSUPPORTED` (the volume cap's rights band disallowed
//     op 0, or `cap_handler_volume` is not wired for this kind).
//     Should never reach a correctly-provisioned mount.pdxfs today;
//     reserved for the day a mint helper lands a volume cap whose
//     narrow dropped R_VOL_INVOKE.
//
//   VDS_ERR_HANDLE   (= 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFE) -- kernel returned
//     `INVOKE_RESULT_INVALID_HANDLE` (slot > 256 or kind-tag zero).
//
// A caller distinguishes success from the four-way refusal by a
// single `cmp rax, 0x10000; jae refuse` -- the real device_slot bound
// (16-bit, well below 0x10000) separates every live answer from every
// sentinel (all of which sit above 0xFFFF...FC00).
//
// ======================================================================
// Encoder-pitfall discipline
// ======================================================================
//
// No `add reg, [mem]` anywhere. No `test` mnemonic (every zero-check
// is `cmp reg, 0`). Reserved-label discipline: every branch label in
// this file is prefixed `mount_vds_`. No multiline `pub let foo = "..."`.
// Module basename `volume_device_slot` matches the file name byte-for-
// byte (recent regression check per the pdx-encoder-pitfalls memory).
//
// ======================================================================
// SC+ IDs used directly by this file
// ======================================================================
//
// SC+ ID 4 (`cap_invoke`) -- the ring-3 syscall ID defined by
// `src/kernel/core/syscall/dispatch.pdx` L614-L615 and L1371-L1375.
// Register convention (standard Linux SYSCALL):
//   rax = 4             SC+ ID
//   rdi = cap_slot      (first SysV arg, which `dispatch_cap_invoke`
//                        shuffles into cap_invoke's rdi)
//   rsi = op_arg        (second SysV arg; low 8 bits = op code,
//                        high 56 bits must be zero per
//                        `cap_handler_volume`'s `op_arg[63:8] == 0`
//                        gate, else `VOL_TAIL_BAD_ARG` = 0xFFFFED22)
// Return in rax: either the live device_slot or one of the sentinels
// above.
//
// Upstream: paideia-os src/kernel/core/cap/kind_volume.pdx
// (`VOL_OP_QUERY_DEVICE_SLOT = 0`, `cap_handler_volume`'s dispatcher);
// paideia-os src/kernel/core/cap/invoke.pdx (`INVOKE_DENIED`,
// `INVOKE_UNSUPPORTED`, `INVOKE_RESULT_INVALID_HANDLE`); paideia-os
// design/pdxfs/sys-mount-abi.md §2.8 (the wire-through requirement
// this file closes).

module VolumeDeviceSlot = structure {

  // ============================================================================
  // Op code (mirrors kernel-side kind_volume.pdx `VOL_OP_QUERY_DEVICE_SLOT`).
  // ============================================================================

  pub let VDS_OP_QUERY_DEVICE_SLOT : u64 = 0

  // ============================================================================
  // SC+ ID 4 — `cap_invoke`.
  // ============================================================================

  pub let VDS_SC_CAP_INVOKE : u64 = 4

  // ============================================================================
  // Return sentinels. See module header's §"Return taxonomy".
  // ============================================================================

  pub let VDS_ERR_DECODE : u64 = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF
  pub let VDS_ERR_HANDLE : u64 = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFE
  pub let VDS_ERR_INVOKE : u64 = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFD
  pub let VDS_ERR_UNSUP  : u64 = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFC

  // ============================================================================
  // Upper bound on a valid device_slot answer. Kernel-side
  // `volume_row_device_slot` masks to 16 bits (`and rax, 0xFFFF`), so a
  // live answer is in [0..0x10000). Every sentinel sits far above this
  // bound (the lowest, `VDS_ERR_UNSUP = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFC`, is well
  // past 2^48). Callers discriminate with `cmp rax, 0x10000; jae
  // refuse`.
  // ============================================================================

  pub let VDS_SLOT_MAX : u64 = 0x10000

  // ==========================================================================
  // volume_device_slot_query(narrowed_volume_slot) -> u64
  //     (device_slot in [0..0x10000) on success;
  //      VDS_ERR_* otherwise -- see module header)
  //
  //   rdi = narrowed_volume_slot  (a KIND_VOLUME cap slot, typically
  //                                 the output of
  //                                 VolumeCap::volume_cap_resolve)
  //
  // Issues cap_invoke(narrowed_volume_slot, op_arg = 0) via SC+ ID 4.
  // op_arg = 0 encodes VOL_OP_QUERY_DEVICE_SLOT (op code = 0, high
  // bits zero per `cap_handler_volume`'s op_arg[63:8]==0 gate).
  //
  // Pure leaf at the syscall boundary. rax/rdi/rsi are all SysV
  // caller-save; the SYSCALL preserves callee-save registers per the
  // amd64 fast-syscall convention, so no push/pop needed. No branch
  // (the kernel's own return value IS the function's return).
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let volume_device_slot_query : (u64) -> u64 !{mem, sysreg} @{cap} =
    fn (narrowed_volume_slot: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem, sysreg}, capabilities: {cap},
      justification: "mount.pdxfs.PFS-11 (paideia-os#2578): volume_device_slot_query entry point. Issues `mov rsi, 0 (VDS_OP_QUERY_DEVICE_SLOT = op code 0, high bits zero so op_arg[63:8]==0 passes kernel's VOL_TAIL_BAD_ARG gate); mov rax, 4 (VDS_SC_CAP_INVOKE); syscall`. rdi arrives holding narrowed_volume_slot (first SysV arg); `dispatch_cap_invoke` shuffles rsi->rdi (slot) and rdx->rsi (op_arg), so our rdi (slot) must stay in rdi and our rsi (op_arg) must stay in rsi -- which is already the Linux SYSCALL convention this file follows. Kernel's `cap_handler_volume` dispatches op=0 to `volume_row_device_slot`, requires R_VOL_INVOKE (0x008) on the cap descriptor's rights band; refusal returns INVOKE_DENIED (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFD). Live row returns device_slot in [0..0x10000) via header bits [39:24] mask. Dead row returns VOL_DECODE_BAD (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF). Pure leaf -- rax/rdi/rsi all SysV caller-save, no push/pop, no branch (the kernel's return IS our return). No `add reg,[mem]`; no `test`. Labels: none (single straight-line block).",
      block: {
        mov rsi, 0;                          // VDS_OP_QUERY_DEVICE_SLOT (op code 0, op_arg[63:8]==0)
        mov rax, 4;                          // VDS_SC_CAP_INVOKE
        syscall;
        ret;
      }
    }
}
